/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Emotions and Relationships: grief, envy, forgiveness, shame, nostalgia, awe, apologies and siblings */
WB.L("grief-how-we-mourn", "emotions", "intermediate", "Grief: what really happens when we lose someone", "The famous five stages of grief were never meant to be a map. Here is what researchers have learned about how people actually carry loss, and why there is no single right way to do it.",
  [
    ["The stages that weren't", "The five stages of grief began as observations of dying patients, not a rulebook for mourners.", "In 1969 the Swiss-American psychiatrist Elisabeth Kubler-Ross published On Death and Dying, describing denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance. She had been listening to people who were terminally ill, not to people who had lost someone. Over time the stages escaped the book and became a kind of checklist, with grieving people sometimes worrying they were doing it wrong because they skipped anger or circled back to denial. Later research has not found that people move through fixed stages in a set order. Kubler-Ross herself later said the stages were never meant to tidy messy emotions into neat packages. They can still be useful words for feelings that turn up, but grief is less a staircase than weather: changeable, overlapping and different for everyone."],
    ["Grief lives in the body", "Loss affects sleep, appetite, concentration and even the heart, not just the mind.", "People who are grieving often describe it physically. A heavy chest, a hollow stomach, exhaustion that sleep does not fix. Many find they cannot concentrate, lose things, forget appointments or reread the same page again and again, a fog sometimes called grief brain. Appetite can vanish or swing the other way. These reactions make sense: losing someone close is a major stress, and the body's stress systems respond accordingly. In rare cases, sudden intense emotional shock is linked to a temporary heart condition called takotsubo cardiomyopathy, nicknamed broken heart syndrome, where part of the heart muscle suddenly weakens. Most people recover from it. The broader point is that grief is not weakness or self-indulgence. It is a whole-body response to a real and painful change in your world."],
    ["Swinging between two worlds", "Healthy grieving often means moving back and forth between facing the loss and getting on with life.", "Imagine a widower who spends a morning crying over old photos, then an afternoon learning to cook the meals his wife used to make, then an evening watching football and briefly forgetting. That back and forth is exactly what the Dutch researchers Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut described in their dual process model of grief. One side is loss-oriented: remembering, yearning, feeling the pain. The other is restoration-oriented: handling practical tasks, trying new roles, taking breaks from sorrow. Their idea is that people cope best when they move between the two, rather than staying locked in either. Laughing at a funeral, or enjoying a good day soon after a death, is not a betrayal. Respite is part of how the mind gradually absorbs something too large to take in all at once."],
    ["Most people are more resilient than they expect", "Research suggests the most common response to loss is a steady, if painful, resilience.", "Psychologist George Bonanno at Columbia University spent years following people after bereavement and other hard events. A striking finding was that the most common pattern was not long collapse followed by slow recovery. Many people felt real sadness and distress, yet kept functioning fairly well at work and with family, and their wellbeing stayed relatively stable over time. This does not mean they did not love the person or did not hurt. It means humans seem to have more capacity to absorb loss than we often assume. Bonanno's work also suggests there is no need to force a particular kind of grief, such as talking it all through, if that is not how you cope. Different people find different paths, and many of those paths lead somewhere bearable."],
    ["Keeping the bond", "Many people stay connected to those they have lost, and that can be healthy rather than a failure to move on.", "For much of the twentieth century, the advice was to let go: to detach from the dead and reinvest your feelings elsewhere. In 1996 a book called Continuing Bonds, edited by Dennis Klass, Phyllis Silverman and Steven Nickman, challenged that idea. It argued that many mourners keep an ongoing relationship with the person who died, and that this is often comforting. Think of someone who talks to a late parent at the cemetery, wears a grandmother's ring, cooks a family recipe on an anniversary, or asks themselves what a lost friend would say about a decision. Across many cultures, rituals honour ancestors and keep their memory alive. The goal of grieving is not to forget. It is to find a new place for the person in a life that continues."],
    ["Losses nobody acknowledges", "Some griefs go unrecognised by others, which can make them harder to carry.", "Not every loss comes with a funeral, sympathy cards or time off work. The American grief researcher Kenneth Doka coined the term disenfranchised grief for losses that are not openly acknowledged or socially supported. Examples include a miscarriage, the death of an ex-partner, the loss of a pet, a friend who died by overdose, or the slow loss of a parent to dementia while they are still alive. People may feel they have no right to be so upset, or others may expect them to bounce back quickly. Naming the loss can help. So can creating your own small ritual, such as planting a tree or writing a letter. If you are supporting someone, simply saying 'I know this matters, and I'm sorry' can mean more than any advice."],
    ["When grief gets stuck, and how to help", "For a small group, grief stays intense for a long time, and professional support can help.", "For most people the sharpest pain softens over months and years, even though the loss is never forgotten. For a minority, intense yearning, disbelief and an inability to re-engage with life persist long after the death. This pattern is now recognised by health authorities as prolonged grief disorder, and there are effective therapies for it. This lesson is general information, not medical advice. If grief is overwhelming, talk to your GP, look up healthdirect for support options, or call Lifeline on 13 11 14 if you are struggling to cope or feel unsafe. When helping a friend, the most useful things are often simple: turning up, mentioning the person's name, bringing a meal, and remembering the hard dates like birthdays and anniversaries, long after everyone else has stopped asking."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Who were Kubler-Ross's five stages originally based on?", ["Bereaved spouses", "People who were terminally ill", "Soldiers returning from war", "Children who had lost a parent"], 1, "Kubler-Ross developed her stages from conversations with dying patients, not from studies of mourners."],
    [1, "What is takotsubo cardiomyopathy nicknamed?", ["Grief brain", "Heartache flu", "Broken heart syndrome", "Mourning fatigue"], 2, "It is a temporary weakening of heart muscle linked to sudden intense stress, often called broken heart syndrome."],
    [2, "According to the dual process model, how do people tend to cope best with grief?", ["By moving between facing the loss and attending to everyday life", "By avoiding all reminders of the person", "By focusing only on the loss until the pain ends", "By returning to work as quickly as possible"], 0, "Stroebe and Schut's model proposes that oscillating between loss and restoration helps people absorb a loss gradually."],
    [4, "What does the idea of continuing bonds suggest?", ["Mourners must fully detach to recover", "Grief always ends after one year", "Talking about the dead slows healing", "Staying connected to the person who died can be healthy"], 3, "The continuing bonds view holds that an ongoing inner relationship with the dead is often comforting and normal."],
    [5, "Which is an example of disenfranchised grief?", ["Mourning a grandparent with a large public funeral", "Grieving a pet that others think you should get over quickly", "Feeling sad at a national memorial service", "Missing a friend who moved overseas for a week"], 1, "Disenfranchised grief is loss that others do not openly acknowledge, such as the death of a beloved pet."]
  ]);

WB.L("jealousy-and-envy", "emotions", "intermediate", "Jealousy and envy: the green-eyed emotions", "They are often treated as the same ugly feeling, but jealousy and envy are different emotions with different messages. Understand them, and they can tell you surprisingly useful things.",
  [
    ["Two different feelings", "Envy is wanting what someone else has; jealousy is fearing you will lose what you have to someone else.", "Your colleague gets the promotion you wanted, and something sour twists inside you. That is envy: a two-person emotion about wanting what another person has, whether it is their job, looks, house or talent. Now picture your partner laughing a little too warmly with someone at a party. That sinking, protective feeling is jealousy: a three-person emotion about the threat of losing a relationship you value to a rival. In everyday speech people use the words interchangeably, saying 'I'm so jealous of your holiday' when they mean envious. Psychologists find the distinction useful because the feelings arise from different situations and push us towards different actions. Envy is about lacking something; jealousy is about guarding something. Knowing which one you are feeling is the first step to handling it well."],
    ["An old and universal monster", "Jealousy and envy appear across cultures and throughout history, from scripture to Shakespeare.", "These emotions are ancient. Envy is listed among the seven deadly sins of Christian tradition, and stories from many cultures warn about the evil eye, the idea that an envious gaze can bring misfortune. In Shakespeare's Othello, the scheming Iago warns Othello to beware of jealousy, calling it the green-eyed monster, and then feeds that very monster until it destroys a marriage and several lives. Anthropologists find jealousy and envy in societies all over the world, which suggests they are part of shared human nature rather than inventions of modern consumer culture. That universality does not make them pleasant, but it does mean feeling them is normal. Having the feeling is not a moral failure. What matters far more is what you do next."],
    ["Why we evolved to feel them", "Both emotions likely helped our ancestors protect relationships and keep up with rivals.", "From an evolutionary point of view, painful emotions often exist because they once solved problems. Evolutionary psychologists such as David Buss argue that jealousy evolved to help people guard valuable relationships against rivals, alerting them to threats and motivating them to protect the bond. Envy may have helped our ancestors notice when others were getting ahead in access to food, status or allies, nudging them to try harder. In small groups where standing mattered for survival, ignoring such gaps could be costly. These explanations are theories, and scientists debate the details. But they offer a helpful reframe: your envious or jealous twinge is your mind flagging something it thinks matters. The trouble comes when the alarm is too sensitive, or when it pushes you towards hostility instead of effort."],
    ["Benign envy and malicious envy", "One kind of envy motivates you to improve, while the other makes you want to drag others down.", "Dutch psychologist Niels van de Ven and colleagues have studied two flavours of envy. Benign envy feels like 'I want that too', and it tends to motivate people to work harder and lift themselves up. Malicious envy feels like 'I wish they didn't have it', and it tends to produce resentment, gossip and a wish to pull the other person down. A close cousin of malicious envy is schadenfreude, a German word for the pleasure people sometimes feel at someone else's misfortune. Research suggests which kind of envy arises depends partly on whether we think the other person deserved their success and whether we believe we could achieve something similar. If success seems earned and attainable, benign envy is more likely. That gives you a lever: focus on what is within your reach."],
    ["The comparison machine", "We judge ourselves by comparing with others, and social media supercharges that habit.", "In 1954 the American psychologist Leon Festinger proposed social comparison theory: people evaluate their abilities and lives partly by comparing themselves with others. Comparing upward, with people who seem better off, can inspire or deflate. Comparing downward can comfort but also breed smugness. Today we carry an endless comparison machine in our pockets. Social media feeds tend to show other people's highlights: the holiday, the renovation, the engagement, the perfect sourdough. You are comparing your behind-the-scenes footage with everyone else's trailer. Studies on social media and wellbeing have produced mixed results, but passive scrolling through others' curated lives is often linked with more envy than actively chatting with friends. If a particular account reliably leaves you feeling worse, muting it is not petty. It is sensible maintenance."],
    ["Jealousy in relationships", "A little jealousy is common, but controlling jealousy is a warning sign rather than a sign of love.", "Mild jealousy in a relationship can be a signal that you care and want reassurance. Talking about it honestly, such as saying 'I felt a bit left out when you spent all night talking to them', often brings couples closer. But jealousy can also become corrosive. Checking a partner's phone, tracking their location, restricting who they see or flying into rages over imagined betrayals are not romantic. They are forms of control, and in Australia coercive control is increasingly recognised as a form of family and domestic violence. If you are experiencing this, or worried about your own behaviour, 1800RESPECT on 1800 737 732 offers confidential support. Healthy relationships rest on trust and open communication. Insecurity is something to work on together or individually, not something a partner should be punished for."],
    ["Turning the feeling into information", "Treating envy and jealousy as data about what you value can make them useful rather than destructive.", "Next time envy bites, try asking what exactly you want. If a friend's new business stings, perhaps you crave more independence or creativity in your own work. That is valuable information, and it points towards action. With jealousy, ask what you are afraid of losing and whether the threat is real or a story your mind is telling. Naming the feeling, even silently, can soften it. Gratitude for what you already have also seems to dampen envy, and genuinely celebrating others' wins can feel surprisingly good once you practise it. Remember too that you rarely see the whole picture of anyone's life. The person you envy may be envying someone else, or quietly envying you. The green-eyed monster shrinks considerably when you look at it in daylight."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the key difference between envy and jealousy?", ["Envy is wanting what others have; jealousy is fearing losing what you have to a rival", "Envy is a positive emotion; jealousy is negative", "Jealousy involves objects; envy involves people", "They mean exactly the same thing"], 0, "Envy is a two-person emotion about lacking; jealousy is a three-person emotion about guarding a relationship."],
    [1, "In which Shakespeare play is jealousy called the green-eyed monster?", ["Hamlet", "Macbeth", "Othello", "King Lear"], 2, "In Othello, Iago warns of jealousy as the green-eyed monster while secretly stoking it."],
    [3, "Benign envy is most likely to arise when:", ["We think the other person cheated", "The success seems deserved and achievable for us too", "We feel pleasure at someone's failure", "We have no interest in what they have"], 1, "Research suggests deserved, attainable success tends to spark motivating benign envy rather than resentment."],
    [4, "Who proposed social comparison theory in 1954?", ["David Buss", "Niels van de Ven", "Sigmund Freud", "Leon Festinger"], 3, "Leon Festinger proposed that people judge themselves partly by comparing with others."],
    [6, "What is a useful question to ask when you feel envious?", ["How can I make them fail?", "Why am I such a bad person?", "What exactly do I want that this reveals?", "How can I hide the feeling forever?"], 2, "Envy can point to what you value and want, which can guide positive action."]
  ]);

WB.L("forgiveness-science", "emotions", "intermediate", "Forgiveness: what it is, and what it isn't", "Forgiving someone does not mean forgetting, excusing or letting them back into your life. Here is what forgiveness actually involves, and why researchers think it can lighten the load.",
  [
    ["Carrying a heavy stone", "Holding on to a grudge can keep a hurt alive long after the event is over.", "Picture someone who, twenty years after a bitter family falling out, still replays the argument in the shower, still feels their jaw tighten when the person's name comes up. The original hurt may have taken five minutes. The resentment has taken two decades. Many people describe a grudge as carrying a stone that gets heavier the longer you hold it. Psychologists call the mental replaying rumination, and it tends to keep anger and distress fresh. Forgiveness, in the research sense, is about gradually setting that stone down. It is not about pretending the stone was never thrown, or that it did not hurt. It is about deciding that the hurt will no longer get to run your inner life."],
    ["What forgiveness is not", "Forgiving is different from excusing, forgetting, trusting again or reconciling.", "A lot of resistance to forgiveness comes from misunderstanding it. Forgiving someone does not mean saying what they did was acceptable. It does not mean forgetting, and it does not mean you must trust them again or have them back in your life. Reconciliation, which means restoring a relationship, takes two people and requires the other person to be safe and trustworthy. Forgiveness can happen entirely inside one person, even if the other never apologises, has moved away or has died. You can forgive someone and still keep firm boundaries, report a crime or end a relationship. Researchers such as Robert Enright describe forgiveness as letting go of resentment and the wish for revenge, and in some cases, eventually, offering goodwill, while still recognising that a real wrong was done."],
    ["What the research suggests", "Studies link forgiveness with lower stress and better wellbeing, though cause and effect is hard to untangle.", "Over the past few decades, psychologists have studied forgiveness in many settings, from couples and families to survivors of serious harm. People who tend to forgive more readily generally report less anxiety, less depression and greater life satisfaction, and some studies find links with lower stress responses. In one kind of experiment, people who imagined holding a grudge showed bigger rises in heart rate and blood pressure than when they imagined forgiving. Structured forgiveness programmes, developed by researchers such as Robert Enright and Everett Worthington, have shown benefits in trials. Still, much of the evidence is correlational, so it is hard to be sure whether forgiving makes people healthier or whether calmer, healthier people find it easier to forgive. Probably a bit of both is going on."],
    ["The REACH model", "Psychologist Everett Worthington offers five steps to help people work towards forgiving.", "Everett Worthington, an American psychologist, developed a widely used approach called REACH. R is for recall the hurt, honestly and without minimising it. E is for empathise, trying to understand what might have driven the other person, not to excuse them but to see them as a whole human. A is for the altruistic gift of forgiveness, often helped by remembering times you yourself have been forgiven. C is for commit, perhaps by writing a letter you never send or telling a trusted friend. H is for hold on, because old feelings will resurface and that does not mean the forgiveness failed. Worthington had a powerful personal reason to study this: his mother was murdered in 1996, and he has written about working to forgive the person responsible."],
    ["Forgiveness on a national scale", "Some societies have tried to use truth-telling and forgiveness to heal after mass harm.", "After apartheid ended in South Africa, the country faced an enormous question: how could people who had suffered terrible abuses live alongside those responsible? One answer was the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, chaired by Archbishop Desmond Tutu in the late 1990s. Victims could tell their stories publicly, and perpetrators could apply for amnesty if they fully disclosed politically motivated crimes. Tutu championed the view that there is no future without forgiveness, which became the title of his book about the process. The commission was praised worldwide and also criticised, with some victims feeling justice was sacrificed. In Australia, the 2008 National Apology to the Stolen Generations showed how public acknowledgement of harm can matter deeply. These examples show that forgiveness is political and social as well as personal."],
    ["The hardest one: forgiving yourself", "Self-forgiveness works best when it includes taking responsibility, not just letting yourself off the hook.", "Sometimes the person we cannot forgive is ourselves. Maybe you said something cruel to a parent before they died, or a mistake at work hurt others. Researchers distinguish genuine self-forgiveness from simply excusing yourself. Pseudo self-forgiveness skips responsibility: 'It wasn't really my fault.' Genuine self-forgiveness starts by owning what you did, feeling appropriate remorse, making amends where possible, and then releasing ongoing self-punishment. This links closely with the idea of self-compassion, popularised by psychologist Kristin Neff: treating yourself with the kindness you would offer a friend who had made the same mistake. Endless self-criticism rarely makes people better. It more often makes them defensive, stuck or avoidant. Accepting that you are a flawed human who can learn and repair is usually a better route to doing better."],
    ["Taking your time", "Forgiveness is a process that cannot be forced, and it should never be demanded of people who are still unsafe.", "Forgiveness is not a switch you flick. For serious harms, it can take years, and some people decide not to forgive certain things, which is their right. Pressuring someone to forgive, especially a person who has been abused, can add to their pain and can even keep them in danger. Safety and justice come first. If you are experiencing abuse or feel unsafe, 1800RESPECT (1800 737 732) offers confidential support, and in an emergency call 000. For everyday hurts, though, many people find that small steps help: noticing when you are rehearsing the grievance, wondering what was going on for the other person, and choosing to stop feeding the story. This lesson offers general information, not therapy. If an old hurt is still dominating your life, a psychologist or counsellor can help, and your GP can discuss options. Forgiving is ultimately something you do for your own freedom."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Which statement about forgiveness is true according to researchers?", ["Forgiving means you must reconcile with the person", "You can forgive someone and still keep firm boundaries", "Forgiveness requires the other person to apologise", "Forgiving means forgetting what happened"], 1, "Forgiveness is an internal letting go of resentment and is separate from reconciliation or trust."],
    [2, "Why is it hard to say forgiveness causes better health?", ["No studies have looked at it", "Forgiving people are always unhealthy", "Much of the evidence is correlational", "Health has nothing to do with emotions"], 2, "Correlational studies cannot easily show whether forgiving improves health or healthier people forgive more easily."],
    [3, "In Worthington's REACH model, what does the H stand for?", ["Hold on to forgiveness", "Hurt the offender", "Hide your feelings", "Heal instantly"], 0, "H means holding on, since old feelings may resurface without meaning forgiveness has failed."],
    [4, "Who chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission?", ["Nelson Mandela", "Kofi Annan", "F. W. de Klerk", "Desmond Tutu"], 3, "Archbishop Desmond Tutu chaired the commission in the late 1990s."],
    [5, "What distinguishes genuine self-forgiveness from pseudo self-forgiveness?", ["Genuine self-forgiveness begins with taking responsibility", "Genuine self-forgiveness never involves remorse", "Pseudo self-forgiveness requires making amends", "There is no real difference"], 0, "Genuine self-forgiveness owns the wrong and seeks to repair before letting go of self-punishment."]
  ]);

WB.L("shame-and-guilt", "emotions", "advanced", "Shame and guilt: the self-conscious emotions", "They both make your face burn, but shame and guilt pull you in opposite directions. One tends to help you repair; the other tends to make you hide. Learning the difference can change how you treat yourself and others.",
  [
    ["Two kinds of sting", "Guilt focuses on what you did, while shame focuses on who you are.", "Imagine you forget a close friend's birthday. One inner voice says, 'I did a thoughtless thing, I should call and make it up to her.' Another says, 'I'm a terrible friend, I'm hopeless, I always let people down.' Both are painful, but they are different emotions. Psychologist June Tangney and her colleagues, who have studied this for decades, put it neatly: guilt is about a bad thing I did, while shame is about being a bad self. The behaviour is the same, but the focus differs. Guilt targets a specific action that can be changed. Shame targets the whole person, which feels far harder to fix. That small shift in focus turns out to have big consequences for what people do next."],
    ["Emotions that need an audience", "Shame, guilt, embarrassment and pride are called self-conscious emotions because they involve judging ourselves through others' eyes.", "Fear and joy show up very early in life, but shame, guilt, embarrassment and pride seem to emerge later, typically during the toddler years, once children develop a sense of themselves as separate people who can be evaluated. Researchers call these the self-conscious emotions. They involve stepping outside yourself and imagining how you look to others, or measuring yourself against a standard. That is why they are so tied to social life. Charles Darwin was fascinated by blushing, which he saw as a uniquely human expression linked to self-attention and others' opinions. These emotions likely helped our ancestors live in groups, nudging people to follow shared rules, repair damage and avoid being rejected. They are uncomfortable by design: they exist to make us care about how we treat others."],
    ["Guilt tends to repair", "Because guilt focuses on a fixable action, it often motivates apology and making amends.", "In many studies, people who tend to feel guilt, rather than shame, when they mess up are more likely to apologise, accept responsibility and try to set things right. Guilt says the problem is the action, so the solution is a better action. Tangney's research has linked guilt-proneness with empathy and with lower rates of some harmful behaviours later in life. That does not mean more guilt is always better. Excessive, chronic guilt about things that were not your fault can be exhausting, and some people carry guilt for harms they never caused, such as survivors of accidents. But in its healthy form, guilt acts like a moral compass. It points to the gap between what you did and what you value, and invites you to close it."],
    ["Shame tends to hide or lash out", "Shame often leads to withdrawal, secrecy or even blaming and anger towards others.", "When shame strikes, people often want to disappear. They avoid eye contact, hunch their shoulders, stop answering messages, or hide the mistake. Shame says the problem is you, and since you cannot easily swap yourself out, the instinct is to escape. Research has also linked shame-proneness with defensiveness and anger. When someone feels exposed as inadequate, one way to protect against the pain is to turn it outward: 'It's not my fault, it's yours.' This is why shaming people, whether a child, an employee or a stranger online, often backfires. Instead of prompting change, it can trigger hiding, denial or hostility. Researcher and author Brene Brown popularised this idea, arguing that shame thrives on secrecy and silence, and loses some of its grip when spoken about."],
    ["Shame cultures and guilt cultures", "Some scholars once divided societies into shame and guilt cultures, but the idea is now seen as too simple.", "In 1946 the American anthropologist Ruth Benedict published The Chrysanthemum and the Sword, a study of Japanese culture. She contrasted shame cultures, where behaviour is governed mainly by fear of public disgrace, with guilt cultures, where people are guided by an internal conscience. Her framework was influential, but later scholars criticised it as overgeneralised and too neat, and it was written without visiting Japan, partly from wartime sources. Today researchers recognise that people everywhere feel both shame and guilt. Cultures do differ in how emotions are expressed and which situations trigger them, and in some communities family honour or group reputation carries more weight. But no society runs on only one. The deeper lesson is that self-conscious emotions are shaped by culture as well as biology."],
    ["Online shaming and public pile-ons", "Social media has made public shaming faster and bigger, often with consequences far out of proportion.", "Public shaming is ancient. Communities once used stocks, pillories and public notices to humiliate wrongdoers. Social media has revived the practice at enormous scale. A clumsy joke or an angry moment caught on camera can be shared millions of times within hours, and the target may face abuse, lose a job and find their name permanently linked to the worst moment of their life. Writer Jon Ronson explored such cases in his 2015 book So You've Been Publicly Shamed. Accountability for genuine harm matters, but shaming crowds rarely calibrate the punishment to the offence, and the shame-anger cycle can make people dig in rather than reflect. Before joining a pile-on, it is worth asking whether you are aiming for repair, or just enjoying the feeling of righteousness."],
    ["Turning shame into something useful", "Separating your actions from your worth, and practising self-compassion, can shift shame towards healthy guilt.", "The good news is that the shame and guilt split is partly about how we talk to ourselves, which can be practised. When you catch yourself thinking 'I'm an idiot', try rephrasing it as 'I made a mistake, and here's what I can do about it.' That moves the focus from your whole self to a specific action. Self-compassion research suggests that responding to failure with kindness, rather than harsh self-attack, makes people more likely to take responsibility and improve, not less. Talking to a trusted person also helps, because shame tends to shrink when shared and met with understanding. This lesson is general information, not therapy. If shame feels constant or overwhelming, a GP or psychologist can help, healthdirect lists mental health support services across Australia, and Lifeline (13 11 14) is there any time if you are struggling to cope or feel unsafe."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to June Tangney's framing, what is the core difference between guilt and shame?", ["Guilt is about others; shame is about yourself", "Guilt is about a bad thing I did; shame is about being a bad self", "Guilt is physical; shame is mental", "Shame is always healthy; guilt is not"], 1, "Guilt focuses on a specific action, while shame focuses on the whole self."],
    [1, "Why are shame and guilt called self-conscious emotions?", ["They only occur when alone", "They appear at birth", "They involve judging ourselves through others' eyes or against standards", "They are always conscious decisions"], 2, "These emotions require a sense of self that can be evaluated by others or by personal standards."],
    [3, "What is a common response to shame, according to research?", ["Withdrawal, defensiveness or anger", "Immediate apology and repair", "Increased empathy for others", "Feeling calm and relaxed"], 0, "Shame tends to drive hiding or lashing out because it feels like the whole self is the problem."],
    [4, "What is a key criticism of Ruth Benedict's shame and guilt cultures idea?", ["It focused only on Australia", "It claimed nobody feels guilt", "It was based on brain scans", "It was too neat and overgeneralised"], 3, "Later scholars argued the division was too simple, as people everywhere feel both emotions."],
    [6, "Which self-talk is more likely to shift shame towards healthy guilt?", ["I'm a complete failure", "I made a mistake, and here's what I can do about it", "Nobody can ever know about this", "It was entirely someone else's fault"], 1, "Focusing on a specific, fixable action rather than your whole worth encourages repair."]
  ]);

WB.L("nostalgia-science", "emotions", "beginner", "Nostalgia: why we long for the good old days", "Once considered a dangerous disease, nostalgia turns out to be one of the mind's quiet comforts. Here is why an old song can make you feel warmer, more connected and more like yourself.",
  [
    ["A disease of homesick soldiers", "Nostalgia was named in 1688 as a medical condition affecting people far from home.", "In 1688 a Swiss medical student named Johannes Hofer wrote about a strange illness affecting young people far from home, including Swiss soldiers serving abroad. Sufferers were listless, lost their appetite and pined obsessively for their native mountains. Hofer coined a word for it by combining the Greek nostos, meaning homecoming, with algos, meaning pain. Nostalgia, then, literally meant the pain of longing to return home. For a long time doctors treated it as a serious condition, and some blamed everything from changes in air pressure to the clanging of cowbells for triggering it. Over the following centuries the meaning shifted from homesickness for a place to a sentimental longing for the past. And in recent decades, researchers have discovered that nostalgia is usually far more helpful than harmful."],
    ["A bittersweet feeling", "Nostalgia mixes happiness with a touch of loss, and the happy part usually wins.", "Think of hearing a song that was playing during your first summer job, or smelling the same sunscreen your family used on beach holidays. A wave of warmth rolls in, tinged with a faint ache because those days are gone. Psychologists describe nostalgia as a bittersweet emotion, but research led by Constantine Sedikides and Tim Wildschut at the University of Southampton suggests it is predominantly positive. When people write about nostalgic memories, the stories often follow a redemption pattern: they start with something hard or ordinary and end on a positive note. Nostalgic memories also tend to feature the people we love: family, friends, partners. They are rarely about being alone. That social quality turns out to be central to why nostalgia makes us feel good."],
    ["What nostalgia does for us", "Studies suggest nostalgia boosts feelings of connection, meaning and continuity of self.", "In a series of experiments, researchers have prompted people to recall a nostalgic memory and then measured how they felt. Compared with recalling an ordinary memory, nostalgia tended to increase feelings of being loved and supported, raise self-esteem, strengthen a sense that life has meaning, and make people feel more optimistic about the future. One especially interesting effect is self-continuity: the sense that the person you are today is connected to the person you were years ago. In times of change, such as moving cities, retiring or becoming a parent, that thread can be steadying. In a way, nostalgia reminds you of who you are and who has loved you, which can give you the confidence to face what comes next."],
    ["A built-in mood repair kit", "People often turn to nostalgia when they feel lonely, bored or unsettled, and it can help them recover.", "Researchers have found that negative feelings, especially loneliness, tend to trigger nostalgia. When people feel isolated, their minds often drift to memories of being close to others, which seems to restore a sense of connection. Boredom and uncertainty can also prompt nostalgic reflection, perhaps as a way of reaching for meaning. Some studies have even linked nostalgia with physical warmth: in one set of experiments, people felt more nostalgic on colder days, and recalling nostalgic memories seemed to make people feel warmer and more tolerant of cold. The findings are intriguing rather than definitive, but they fit a broader pattern. Nostalgia seems to work like an internal comfort blanket, something the mind reaches for when the present feels a bit chilly."],
    ["Why your teens and twenties loom large", "We remember events from roughly our teens to our late twenties more vividly than other periods.", "Ask older adults to recall important memories or name their favourite music, and a pattern emerges. Memories cluster disproportionately between about age ten and thirty, a phenomenon psychologists call the reminiscence bump. Several explanations have been proposed. That period is packed with firsts: first love, first job, first time living away from home. It is also when people form their adult identity, so events from those years become part of their life story. The brain may encode novel, emotional experiences especially well. This is why the songs of your youth hit differently and why so many people believe music was better when they were young. It probably was not better. It was simply woven into the most formative chapter of your life."],
    ["Nostalgia sells", "Marketers, film studios and politicians all know that nostalgia is persuasive.", "Notice how often old brands relaunch retro packaging, how many films reboot franchises from decades ago, or how songs from the 1980s and 1990s soundtrack modern advertisements. That is not an accident. Nostalgia makes people feel safe and warm, and some research suggests nostalgic feelings can make people more willing to spend money, perhaps because feeling socially connected reduces the importance of holding on to cash. Politicians also tap into nostalgia, promising a return to a better past. It is worth staying alert to this. The good old days are often remembered selectively, with hardships trimmed away and the voices of people who had a much worse time left out. A nostalgic glow is lovely, but it is not always a reliable history book."],
    ["Using nostalgia well", "Nostalgia works best as a source of comfort and direction, not as a place to live.", "There can be a downside. Some people get stuck comparing a disappointing present with an idealised past, a habit sometimes called rosy retrospection. If nostalgia turns into constant regret or a belief that nothing good lies ahead, it stops helping. The trick is to use it as fuel rather than a refuge. Researchers suggest nostalgia can inspire people to reconnect with old friends, revisit values they have drifted from, or recreate rituals they loved. Practically, that might mean making a playlist of songs from your school years, cooking a grandparent's recipe, or messaging an old friend to share a memory. You might even think about tomorrow's nostalgia: the ordinary moments today that, years from now, you will look back on with that same bittersweet warmth."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do the Greek roots of the word nostalgia mean?", ["Old music", "Homecoming and pain", "Memory and joy", "Past and future"], 1, "Hofer combined nostos, homecoming, and algos, pain, in 1688."],
    [1, "What do nostalgic memories most often feature?", ["Being alone", "Work achievements", "Loved ones and close relationships", "Famous people"], 2, "Nostalgic memories are typically social, centred on family, friends and partners."],
    [3, "Which feeling especially tends to trigger nostalgia?", ["Loneliness", "Hunger", "Physical pain", "Excitement"], 0, "Research finds loneliness often prompts nostalgia, which can restore a sense of connection."],
    [4, "What is the reminiscence bump?", ["A head injury that causes memory loss", "A drop in memory after age fifty", "A surge of nostalgia on birthdays", "A cluster of vivid memories from roughly the teens to late twenties"], 3, "People tend to recall more memories from about ages ten to thirty."],
    [6, "What is rosy retrospection?", ["Remembering the past as better than it really was", "Forgetting childhood completely", "A type of memory test", "Planning future holidays"], 0, "Rosy retrospection is the tendency to idealise the past compared with the present."]
  ]);

WB.L("science-of-awe", "emotions", "beginner", "Awe: the emotion that makes you feel small, in a good way", "Standing under a star-filled sky, hearing a choir swell, watching a stranger's act of courage. Awe is one of the least studied emotions, yet it may be one of the most powerful for our wellbeing.",
  [
    ["Under a desert sky", "Awe is the feeling of encountering something vast that stretches your understanding.", "Picture lying on red earth in outback Australia, far from any town, as the Milky Way spills across the sky in a band so bright it casts a faint glow. You might feel tiny, a little dizzy, and strangely calm. That is awe. For a long time, psychologists paid it little attention, focusing instead on emotions such as fear and anger. That changed in 2003, when psychologists Dacher Keltner and Jonathan Haidt published an influential paper defining awe as a response to vastness, something much larger than the self, combined with a need for accommodation, meaning your mental frameworks have to stretch to make sense of it. It can come from nature, art, music, ideas or people. Awe is the emotion of wonder at the edges of understanding."],
    ["The small self", "Awe tends to shrink our sense of self-importance, which can feel freeing rather than diminishing.", "One of the most consistent findings in awe research is what psychologists call the small self. When people experience awe, they often describe feeling smaller, and in experiments they sometimes even draw themselves smaller when asked to sketch themselves. That might sound unpleasant, but most people find it relieving. Everyday worries, from emails to petty arguments, seem to shrink alongside the self. Brain imaging studies have suggested awe may quieten activity in parts of the brain associated with self-focused thinking, though this work is still developing. The result is a feeling of being part of something larger: a community, a species, a universe. Many people describe awe as making them feel connected rather than isolated, humbled but not humiliated."],
    ["Awe makes us kinder", "Experiments suggest awe can make people more generous and helpful towards others.", "In one memorable study, researchers led by Paul Piff had participants spend a minute gazing up at a grove of towering eucalyptus trees on a university campus in California, while others looked at a plain building. Afterwards, someone nearby stumbled and dropped a handful of pens. People who had been looking at the tall trees picked up more pens to help. Other experiments have found that inducing awe, through videos of natural wonders for example, made people more generous in sharing resources. A plausible explanation is the small self effect: when your own concerns feel less pressing, other people's needs come into sharper focus. Awe seems to tilt us, at least briefly, away from self-interest and towards cooperation. There is a pleasing irony in Australian eucalypts helping science discover that."],
    ["Your body on awe", "Awe has its own physical signature, including goosebumps and a slower, calmer heart.", "Have you ever felt shivers run down your spine during a powerful piece of music, or the hair on your arms rise at the top of a mountain? Goosebumps and chills are commonly reported during awe. Some researchers suggest awe is linked with activity in the vagus nerve, part of the system that helps calm the body, and people often report slower breathing, widened eyes and a dropped jaw, the classic expression of amazement. One line of research has linked frequent positive emotions, awe among them, with lower levels of inflammatory markers in the body, though such findings are early and should not be overstated. What seems clear is that awe is not just a thought. It is a whole-body experience, and that makes it noticeably different from simple happiness or excitement."],
    ["The view from space", "Many astronauts describe a profound shift in perspective called the overview effect.", "When astronauts look back at Earth from orbit, many report a powerful experience: seeing the planet as a fragile blue ball suspended in black space, with no visible borders between nations. In 1987 the writer Frank White coined the term overview effect for this cognitive shift. Astronauts have described a sudden sense of the unity of life on Earth, concern for protecting the planet, and impatience with human conflicts that look trivial from that height. The famous Earthrise photograph taken by Apollo 8 astronauts in 1968, showing Earth rising over the Moon's horizon, gave many people on the ground a taste of this feeling. You do not need a rocket, though. Awe researchers find that smaller, everyday experiences can produce a similar, if gentler, shift in perspective."],
    ["Everyday sources of awe", "Awe does not require mountains or space travel; it often comes from other people.", "We tend to associate awe with grand landscapes like Uluru, the Great Barrier Reef or the Grand Canyon. But Dacher Keltner's research, gathering stories of awe from people in many countries, suggests that one of the most common sources is other people, particularly witnessing courage, kindness or overcoming adversity, something he calls moral beauty. Other frequent sources include nature, collective experiences like singing together or a stadium crowd roaring, music, visual art, spiritual or religious experiences, and big ideas, such as suddenly grasping how old the universe is. Life and death also inspire awe: holding a newborn baby or sitting with someone at the end of their life. Awe, in other words, is all around us. The challenge is noticing it amid busy routines."],
    ["Making room for more awe", "Simple habits like awe walks can bring more of this emotion into daily life.", "In a study published in 2020, researchers asked older adults to take a weekly fifteen-minute walk. Some were simply told to walk, while others were encouraged to take an awe walk, deliberately looking for things that amazed them, from the intricate pattern of a leaf to the sweep of clouds. Over eight weeks, the awe walkers reported more positive emotions and less daily distress, and in photos they took of themselves, they increasingly made themselves smaller in the frame and smiled more. You can try a version of this anywhere. Look up at the night sky, watch the sunrise, listen to a piece of music with full attention, or read about the scale of the universe. Awe seems to be a free, renewable resource that most of us underuse."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to Keltner and Haidt, awe involves vastness and what else?", ["A sense of danger", "A need to stretch our understanding", "Physical exhaustion", "Feeling superior"], 1, "Awe combines perceived vastness with a need for accommodation, meaning our mental frameworks must stretch."],
    [2, "In Paul Piff's tree study, what did people who looked at tall eucalyptus trees do?", ["Picked up more dropped pens to help", "Became more competitive", "Took longer to answer questions", "Felt more anxious"], 0, "Participants who gazed at the trees helped more by picking up more pens."],
    [4, "What is the overview effect?", ["A fear of heights in pilots", "A camera technique for photographing Earth", "A shift in perspective astronauts report when seeing Earth from space", "A theory about galaxy formation"], 2, "Frank White coined the term in 1987 for the profound shift astronauts often report."],
    [5, "According to Keltner's research, one of the most common sources of awe is:", ["Expensive purchases", "Winning competitions", "Social media", "Other people's courage and kindness"], 3, "Keltner found moral beauty, witnessing others' goodness, was a very common source of awe."],
    [6, "What did participants on awe walks report over eight weeks?", ["More boredom", "More positive emotions and less daily distress", "Worse sleep", "No change at all"], 1, "Awe walkers reported more positive emotions and less distress than regular walkers."]
  ]);

WB.L("art-of-apology", "emotions", "beginner", "How to say sorry: the art of a good apology", "Few words carry as much power as 'I'm sorry', and few are so often fumbled. Here is what research and real life reveal about apologies that actually heal, and the ones that make things worse.",
  [
    ["Why apologies matter", "A sincere apology can repair trust, calm anger and help both people move on.", "Think about a time someone genuinely apologised to you. Often something softens almost immediately: the anger loosens, the story you were telling yourself about them shifts. A good apology does important social work. It acknowledges that a norm was broken, shows the person who caused harm understands it, and signals they value the relationship enough to swallow some pride. Research on apologies has found that a sincere apology can reduce anger and make forgiveness more likely, while a poor one, or none at all, can deepen the wound. Yet many of us find apologising surprisingly hard. Admitting fault can feel like losing status or inviting blame. Understanding what makes an apology work can make it easier to do well, and to do sooner."],
    ["The ingredients of a good apology", "Effective apologies usually include acknowledgement, responsibility, regret and an offer to make things right.", "Researchers and counsellors broadly agree on the core parts of a strong apology. First, name what you did, specifically: 'I interrupted you repeatedly in that meeting', rather than a vague 'Sorry about earlier.' Second, take responsibility without shifting blame. Third, express genuine regret and show you understand how it affected the other person. Fourth, offer to repair the damage where possible, and commit to doing better. Some apologies also include a brief explanation, though this needs care, as explanations easily slide into excuses. One study of apology elements led by Roy Lewicki in the United States found that acknowledging responsibility was the most important ingredient, closely followed by an offer of repair. In other words, people care most about you owning it and fixing it."],
    ["The non-apology", "Some apologies only look like apologies, and they often make people angrier.", "'I'm sorry if anyone was offended.' 'Mistakes were made.' 'I'm sorry you feel that way.' These are classic non-apologies. They use the language of sorry while dodging responsibility. The word 'if' implies the harm may not be real. The passive 'mistakes were made' hides who made them. 'I'm sorry you feel that way' places the problem in the other person's feelings rather than your actions. Another common trap is the 'sorry, but' apology, where everything after the 'but' undoes what came before. People generally see through these quickly, and research suggests poor apologies can be worse than saying nothing, because they add an insult to the original injury. If you find yourself reaching for 'if' or 'but', pause and ask what you actually did."],
    ["A nation says sorry", "On 13 February 2008, Australia's Parliament apologised to the Stolen Generations.", "Apologies can matter on a national scale too. On 13 February 2008, Prime Minister Kevin Rudd delivered the National Apology to Australia's Indigenous peoples, particularly the Stolen Generations, the many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children forcibly removed from their families under government policies across much of the twentieth century. Rudd's speech, made in federal Parliament, said sorry three times: for the pain, suffering and hurt of the Stolen Generations and their families; to the mothers and fathers, the brothers and sisters, for the breaking up of families and communities; and for the indignity and degradation inflicted on a proud people and a proud culture. Crowds gathered across the country to watch on big screens, and many people wept. The apology followed a 1997 national inquiry report called Bringing Them Home, which had recommended one. For many, the words carried enormous weight because they acknowledged real harm openly, after years in which a formal apology had been refused."],
    ["Sorry is not always an admission of liability", "In Australia, laws in every state and territory generally protect simple apologies from being used as admissions of legal fault.", "Some people avoid apologising after an accident because they worry it will be used against them. In Australia, every state and territory has passed laws, often within civil liability legislation, that generally protect apologies from being treated as admissions of fault in many civil cases. The idea is to encourage the human decency of saying sorry, for example after a mishap, without fear that a few kind words will decide a court case. The details differ between jurisdictions and some types of cases are treated differently, so this is general information, not legal advice. Separately, many insurance policies, including car insurance, ask you not to admit fault, so check your policy. If you are worried about a specific situation, check with a lawyer or your local legal aid service. Doctors and hospitals are also encouraged to practise open disclosure when things go wrong."],
    ["The over-apologiser", "Apologising for everything can dilute your apologies and undermine your confidence.", "At the other extreme are people who say sorry constantly: when someone bumps into them, before asking a question, when sending a perfectly reasonable email. Chronic over-apologising can make you seem less confident, and it can water down the meaning of a genuine apology when one is needed. It can also subtly shift responsibility onto yourself for things that are not your fault. A useful trick is to swap some sorrys for thank yous. Instead of 'Sorry I'm late', try 'Thanks for waiting.' Instead of 'Sorry to bother you', try 'Thanks for your help with this.' The first version focuses on your fault; the second recognises the other person's kindness. Save sorry for when you have actually done something that harmed or inconvenienced someone."],
    ["Receiving an apology well", "Accepting an apology graciously, without pretending it did not matter, helps both people move forward.", "Apologies take two people, and receiving one well is a skill too. A common reflex is to wave it away with 'Oh, it's fine, don't worry about it', even when it was not fine. That can leave the hurt unacknowledged. A more honest response might be, 'Thank you for saying that. It did upset me, and I appreciate you recognising it.' This validates the apology while being truthful. You are not obliged to forgive on the spot, and it is fine to say you need a little time. On the other hand, if someone has genuinely apologised and made amends, repeatedly bringing the incident back up can stop a relationship from healing. Good apologies, well received, often leave relationships stronger than before, because both people have proven they can repair."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "According to Roy Lewicki's research, which apology ingredient matters most?", ["A detailed explanation", "Acknowledging responsibility", "Asking for forgiveness", "Expressing emotion through tears"], 1, "The study found acknowledging responsibility was the most important element, followed by an offer of repair."],
    [2, "Which of these is a classic non-apology?", ["I'm sorry I forgot your birthday", "I'm sorry I raised my voice at you", "I'm sorry if anyone was offended", "I'm sorry I broke your vase, I'll replace it"], 2, "The word 'if' implies the harm may not be real and avoids responsibility."],
    [3, "Which Prime Minister delivered the National Apology to the Stolen Generations in 2008?", ["Kevin Rudd", "John Howard", "Paul Keating", "Julia Gillard"], 0, "Kevin Rudd delivered the apology in federal Parliament on 13 February 2008."],
    [5, "What is a suggested swap for over-apologisers?", ["Never speak in meetings", "Apologise twice as often", "Use more formal language", "Replace some sorrys with thank yous"], 3, "Saying 'thanks for waiting' instead of 'sorry I'm late' recognises others without taking unnecessary blame."],
    [6, "What is a healthy way to receive a sincere apology?", ["Insist it was totally fine even if it was not", "Thank them and be honest that it mattered", "Refuse to respond", "Bring it up again in every argument"], 1, "Acknowledging both the apology and the hurt is honest and helps the relationship heal."]
  ]);

WB.L("siblings-science", "emotions", "beginner", "Siblings: the longest relationship of your life", "Your brothers and sisters often know you longer than your parents, partner or children. Here is what research says about rivalry, birth order myths, only children and why sibling bonds matter so much.",
  [
    ["The longest relationship", "For many people, siblings are the relationship that lasts longest across a whole lifetime.", "Think about who might be around for your entire life. Parents usually die before us, and partners and friends tend to arrive later. Siblings, though, can share almost every chapter, from childhood bath times to retirement and beyond. Most people around the world grow up with at least one brother or sister, and for many those relationships span seven or eight decades. Siblings share parents, homes, family jokes and memories nobody else holds. They can be best friends, fierce rivals, or both on the same day. Researchers have long focused on parents and partners, but there is growing interest in siblings, partly because these relationships shape how we learn to share, fight, negotiate and love, and because they often matter more in old age than people expect."],
    ["Rivalry and its roots", "Sibling rivalry often stems from competition for parents' attention and perceived unfairness.", "Two young brothers arguing over who gets the bigger slice of cake is a scene played out in households everywhere. Sibling conflict is extremely common in childhood, and observational studies of young siblings at home have recorded squabbles happening many times a day. Much of it is about scarce resources, especially parents' time, attention and approval. Research suggests one of the biggest drivers of lasting resentment is not conflict itself, but differential treatment: when children believe a parent favours one sibling over another. Interestingly, what matters most is often whether children see the difference as fair. A younger child getting more help with homework because they need it may be accepted, while unexplained favouritism tends to linger. Parents who explain their reasons and avoid comparisons can help reduce rivalry."],
    ["A training ground for life", "Growing up with siblings gives children constant practice at conflict, cooperation and empathy.", "For all the squabbling, sibling relationships can be a powerful training ground. Children with siblings get daily practice at sharing, taking turns, standing up for themselves, reading others' moods and making up after fights. Older siblings often teach younger ones, from tying shoelaces to navigating school, and teaching seems to benefit both sides. Some research suggests that warm sibling relationships in childhood are linked with better emotional understanding and social skills, though family environment as a whole plays a big role. Siblings also act as a buffer: in families going through stress, such as divorce, a supportive sibling can help children cope. Of course, not every sibling relationship is positive, and ongoing physical or emotional bullying by a sibling is harmful and deserves to be taken seriously, not dismissed as normal rivalry."],
    ["The birth order myth", "Large studies have found little evidence that birth order shapes personality in the way popular culture claims.", "Popular wisdom says firstborns are bossy, responsible achievers, middle children are peacemakers who feel overlooked, and youngest children are charming, rebellious and spoiled. It is a satisfying story, and most of us can think of examples. But when researchers analyse large samples, the effects mostly vanish. A 2015 study by German psychologists Julia Rohrer, Boris Egloff and Stefan Schmukle examined data from over twenty thousand people in Germany, the United States and the United Kingdom. They found no meaningful birth order effects on personality traits such as extraversion, agreeableness or conscientiousness. They did find a very small average advantage for firstborns on intelligence measures, too small to matter for any individual. Birth order stereotypes seem to persist because they make tidy stories, and we remember the examples that fit."],
    ["Why siblings can be so different", "Siblings often differ greatly because each experiences the family in their own way.", "Many parents marvel at how different their children are, despite sharing the same home and genes from the same parents. Behavioural genetics helps explain this. Siblings share, on average, about half of their genetic variation, so there is plenty of room for difference. And surprisingly, research suggests that much of the environmental influence on personality comes from what scientists call the non-shared environment: experiences that differ between siblings, such as different friends, teachers, hobbies, illnesses or even different treatment by parents. A family is not one environment but several, because each child arrives at a different time, with a different temperament, and parents themselves change over the years. Siblings may also deliberately carve out different niches, one becoming the sporty one and another the bookish one, to stand out."],
    ["What about only children?", "The stereotype of the lonely, spoiled only child is not well supported by research.", "Only children have long carried a bad reputation. In the late nineteenth century, an American psychologist named Granville Stanley Hall reportedly called being an only child a disease in itself, and the stereotype of the spoiled, selfish, lonely only child stuck. But research since then, including reviews by American psychologist Toni Falbo, has generally found that only children are much like children with siblings on measures of personality, sociability and adjustment. Where differences show up, they tend to be small, and some favour only children, such as slightly higher achievement, perhaps because they receive more parental attention and resources. As families get smaller in Australia and many other countries, only children are becoming more common. Children without siblings still gain social skills through cousins, friends, school and community."],
    ["Siblings in adulthood", "Sibling relationships often shift in adulthood, becoming closer again in later life for many people.", "After people leave home, sibling contact often drops as careers, partners and children take centre stage. Yet many sibling relationships warm again in midlife and old age. Shared challenges, such as caring for ageing parents, can bring siblings together, though they can also reignite old tensions over fairness, money and who does the most. Later in life, siblings are often among the few people left who remember your childhood, and many older adults find these bonds a valuable source of companionship and support. Some sibling relationships do break down entirely, and estrangement can be painful even when it feels necessary. If you have a sibling you have drifted from, a small step, such as sharing an old photo or a memory, can sometimes reopen the door to the longest relationship you will ever have."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why are sibling relationships often called the longest of a person's life?", ["Siblings always live together", "They can span from childhood to old age, outlasting relationships with parents", "Siblings are legally required to stay in contact", "They begin before birth"], 1, "Siblings often share nearly every life stage, frequently outlasting parents and preceding partners."],
    [1, "According to research, what is a major driver of lasting sibling resentment?", ["Perceived favouritism from parents", "Sharing a bedroom", "Having different hobbies", "Age gaps of more than two years"], 0, "Differential treatment, especially when it seems unfair, is strongly linked with lasting resentment."],
    [3, "What did the 2015 study of over twenty thousand people find about birth order?", ["Firstborns are much more extraverted", "Youngest children are far more rebellious", "Middle children are less agreeable", "No meaningful effects on personality traits"], 3, "The study found no meaningful birth order effects on personality and only a tiny intelligence difference."],
    [4, "What is the non-shared environment?", ["The genes siblings do not share", "A house where siblings live apart", "Experiences that differ between siblings in the same family", "The school environment only"], 2, "Different friends, teachers and experiences shape siblings differently, even within one family."],
    [5, "What has research generally found about only children?", ["They are much more selfish", "They are similar to children with siblings in personality and adjustment", "They cannot develop social skills", "They are always lonely"], 1, "Reviews such as Toni Falbo's found only children are much like others, with few and small differences."]
  ]);
